I'm stuck with a Windows box right now. I've used Linux in the past
and would like to use it again.

A related question: how many people began using either tilesets or
ASCII, switched, and now play exclusively using the interface they
*didn't* learn on?

I ask because I have a hunch that most people simply get used to the
version they learned on, and from that time on, the other just doesn't
"feel" right. My Debian box came with a precompiled version that
defaulted to tiles (and wouldn't toggle to ASCII easily), so that's
how I learned. After joining this group, I decided to try ASCII for a
while, since that seemed to be what most of the "hardcore" players
used. I did it for a short while, then switched back. I like the
tiles.

I think the issue of "the tiles are ugly" or "my imagination is better
than any graphic" is a bit beside the point. Once you've gotten used
to the game, you're responding to what you know the letter/tile
represents, not the way it looks--regardless of what version you use.
